// Which way round the desktop is drawn.
//
// The fourth writing of one closed set: `domicile_protocol::Theme` is what the
// compositor serializes, `components/domicile/common/theme.h` is what the
// browser process parses that against, this is what the page's own type system
// knows, and `themeSchema` in `@domicile/chrome-sdk` is what a shell reads.
//
// AN ENUM RATHER THAN A `DOMString` BECAUSE IT CROSSES IN BOTH DIRECTIONS,
// which is what makes it different from `DomicileDisplay.transform` next door.
// That one is read; this one is also *written* -- `setTheme()` takes it -- and
// a `DOMString` parameter would let a shell hand the compositor a word no
// desktop can be, which would travel all the way to the socket before anything
// asked. The bindings refuse it here instead, at the call.
//
// THERE ARE TWO AND THERE IS NO `system`. A page under this engine has no
// system to follow: Domicile is it. `prefers-color-scheme` still answers, as
// it answers in any browser, and a shell that read it would be asking the
// engine what the engine was told -- so the theme is a fact on this interface
// rather than a media query.
//
// ORDER. WebIDL attaches no meaning to it; it is written in the X-macro's
// order anyway, because `scripts/test-themes-agree.sh` compares all four
// writings as sequences -- `mojom::Theme` is an `enum class : int32_t`
// numbered by position, so two lists that agree on membership and disagree on
// order fail nowhere and paint the desk the wrong way round.

enum DomicileTheme {
  "dark",
  "light",
};
